@4.23 MEM.INC.BL -- a block copied, a block filled
.
=4.23 MEM.INC.BL -- A BLOCK COPIED, A BLOCK FILLED
.
=DESCRIPTION
.
.  MEM.COPY
.      IN: MEM.SOURCE MEM.TARGET MEM.COUNT
.      OUT: MEM.OK
.
.  MEM.FILL
.      IN: MEM.TARGET MEM.COUNT MEM.VALUE
.      OUT: MEM.OK
.
:#INCLUDE "GPB.INC.BL"
:#INCLUDE "MEM.INC.BL"
:
:MEM.TARGET = 4096 : MEM.COUNT = 1000 : MEM.VALUE = 32
:GOSUB MEM.FILL
:IF MEM.OK = 0 THEN PRINT "REFUSED"
.
.The KERNAL's memory_copy and memory_fill. The verbs are MEM.BLOCKCOPY and
.MEM.BLOCKFILL, and the two routines share MEM.TARGET, MEM.COUNT and MEM.OK.
.
.MEM.COUNT is 1 to 65535. Anything outside that is refused and MEM.OK comes
.back 0, never truncated. A longer block goes a chunk at a time; a chunk size
.above $4000 cannot be a #DEFINE, which is signed. STASHVRAMGC.INC.BL (SEC
.4.19) is the worked example.
.
.Regions may overlap with the target below the source.
.
.WARNING: both ends are low RAM below $9F00, or a $9F00-$9FFF I/O port. No
.bank survives the call, so $A000-$BFFF cannot be reached. Move a banked
.block a window at a time.
.
.An address in $9F00-$9FFF is used without being stepped. Point a VERA data
.port at the start of a run and pass $9F23 or $9F24 to move a whole block in
.one call. STASHVRAM.INC.BL (SEC 4.18) does that by hand.
.
.Either routine brings the GP block in, 1,536 bytes, because GP.CALL lives
.there.
.
=SEE ALSO
.
>101|4.19 STASHVRAMGC.INC.BL -- close the holes in a STASHVRAM store
>100|4.18 STASHVRAM.INC.BL -- rectangles and blobs, kept in VRAM
>106|4.23 MEM.INC.BL -- a block copied, a block filled
